g against  the resolution the judgement that you've  made but another part of you is saying  hey this would be pleasant and pleasure  will draw us in we might think of  similar things hitting the snooze button  over and over again well specially if  it's a cold morning and you don't want  to get out of bed in the covers or so so  nice when you know that there's work to  be done that if you don't get done  you're not going to have a nice evening  all right these we can come up with  example after example after example of  people who don't stick to their their  their judgments their decisions and  being able to do that that is part of  temperance so being guided by reason  abiding by it he tells us that we have  to engage in a certain kind of  Prudential reasoning by passing up  certain pleasures we enjoy the highest  pleasure prime example of this would be  you know deciding who to have sex with  or who to even try to pursue a  relationship with maybe it's not a good  idea to take you know whatever comes  along whoever comes along whatever  opportunities come come along and to be  a bit more selective right from an  epicurean perspective that would  probably be the prudent thing to do  similarly when it comes to eating when  it comes to drinking or enjoying drugs  right somebody who imagine you walk into  a place and people have laid out a whole  bunch of different drugs and they're  like you can do anything that you want  here nobody's going to get in trouble  you know nobody's surveilling this area  there's probably some drugs where they  could be quite pleasurable but you you  really ought to turn them down from an  epicurean perspective we also by  enduring pains help ourselves to not  have to deal  with greater pains later on it can be  painful to stick to a dietary regime by  the way as a side note the only diets  that really work long-term are ones that  are that are rational all the gimmick  diets sooner or later fail because  they're not really being tempera